From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server: what an elevated program creates is Administrators', as on Windows (9150)

An elevated program (7601) had the person's token with the person's
default DACL, and every token made the primary group -- this machine's
Domain Users, which every account holds -- the owner of what it creates.
So the keys an elevated installer wrote (HKLM\Software\Classes\.7z) the
same person could change and delete unelevated, and every account owned
every key made at run time: a standard user (bob on qa4) could open
HKLM\Software\Classes and an admin's installer keys for WRITE_DAC and
DELETE, i.e. rewrite every user's file associations.

- Owner, as Windows: Administrators for an administrator's full token
  (elevated, SYSTEM), the user for everyone else (a standard user, an
  administrator unelevated). Token copies keep it; one whose
  Administrators became deny-only falls back to the user.
- An elevated token's default DACL: SYSTEM and Administrators full and
  the users' read (as SYSTEM's), not the person.
- The person keeps their own things: a key an elevated program makes in
  their hive stays without a descriptor (5948), and a key without one now
  keeps the default it reports when a caller sets only part of its
  descriptor (an owner-only SetSecurityInfo no longer gives it the
  caller's default DACL); its key in the person's own
  Installer\UserData\<SID> stays the person's too (per-user packages, 5944).
- A key of a user's hive without a descriptor is checked against the
  user's descriptor, not only reported with it (5950's intent):
  check_object_access fell back to the machine default only, and bob
  created a key in alice's HKCU\Software. That descriptor also gives
  RESTRICTED and ALL APPLICATION PACKAGES read, as Windows' HKCU, so the
  user's own sandboxed programs still read their settings.
- An elevated program's process and threads: owner Administrators,
  SYSTEM and Administrators full, the person only SYNCHRONIZE and the
  limited query. With full access any program the person ran unelevated
  could open their elevated program and write its memory.

Descriptors of keys are not saved, so what elevated programs wrote before
is back to the machine default when the server restarts.

Gate test-elevobj; mutants SG_MUTANT_ELEVATED_DACL_HAS_USER,
SG_MUTANT_OWNER_PRIMARY_GROUP, SG_MUTANT_ELEVATED_PROCESS_OPEN,
SG_MUTANT_USERKEY_UNCHECKED, SG_MUTANT_SETSD_TOKEN_DEFAULTS,
SG_MUTANT_ELEVATED_USERDATA_ADMIN, SG_MUTANT_USERKEY_NO_SANDBOX_READ.
